On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:42:18 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> That's good, so long as one thing is true: the full To/CC list is in the
> one delivered email, right? I think so... I always see when someone has
> CCed other people.

Yes. The recipients a server accepts or drops are the SMTP envelope
(RCPT TO). To and Cc are part of the message itself, and every copy
carries the same headers. Discarding the extra copies changes how many
files land, not what any of them says.

- [fact, OpenSMTPD master, mda.c] At delivery, mda_imsg() adds only
  Return-Path and Delivered-To above the message. It does not touch
  To or Cc.
- [fact, smtp_session.c] smtp_message_begin() adds a Received header.
  smtp_tx_dataline() passes To and Cc through
  header_domain_append_callback(). That callback only appends the
  server's hostname to a bare address with no domain, such as "To:
  pat". An address that already has a domain is left as written. It
  also unfolds the header onto one line, which changes whitespace but
  not addresses.
- [fact, maildir today] maildir-deliver.sh replaces only the Date
  header. Your message I am answering arrived with "To: claude
  <claude@p7x.dev>, lab-devel@p7x.dev", which is what you sent.

> I ask because I want to use that as metadata - was something addressed
> directly to me, or to an agent, etc.

That works, with two limits:

1. Bcc is invisible. smtp_tx_dataline() strips the Bcc header before
   the message is queued, and the dropped envelope copies leave no
   trace. A message that Bcc'd you looks like one that did not
   address you. Nobody on the list uses Bcc now, so this is unlikely
   to matter.

2. Do not use Delivered-To. Under (c) it will name lab-devel@p7x.dev
   on every message, since that is the only copy delivered. To and
   Cc are the headers to read.

notmuch already indexes both. "notmuch search to:pat@patmaddox.com"
matches To and Cc, so the metadata needs no new tagging to start.

> De-duplicating the emails, using only the list recipient, is fine so
> long as the CC list is maintained, which I think it is.

